{{fbdoc item="title" value="gsl, The GNU Scientific Library"}}----
Provides a wide range of mathematical routines such as random number generators, special functions and least-squares fitting.

Website: https://www.gnu.org/software/gsl/, Windows port: http://gnuwin32.sourceforge.net/packages/gsl.htm 
Platforms supported: ""Win32"", ""Win64"", Linux, FreeBSD, Haiku
Headers to include: gsl/*.bi
Header version: 1.16
Examples: in examples/math/GSL/

Haiku R1 beta 6 amd64: `gsl1.bas` and `gsl2.bas` compiled and ran with
FreeBASIC 1.20.4-3 against HaikuPorts GSL 2.6. The Haiku binding links both
`gsl` and `gslcblas`, because the package leaves CBLAS references for the
application link step.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Matrices and precision"}}
	##gsl_matrix_alloc## creates an owning allocation. Check its result, check operations which return a status, and release it with ##gsl_matrix_free## before leaving the example. Matrix views borrow their backing storage and must not be freed as independent owning allocations. GSL uses row-major indexing.

	The math and matrix interfaces use ##Double## values. The examples use ##D0## literal suffixes to specify that precision explicitly. GSL's default error handler aborts on library errors; applications requiring recovery must configure a suitable handler before the relevant calls.

	The installed Win64 GSL 2.8 SDK has been checked against native C matrix layouts, both FreeBASIC backends, nine transpose values and the original examples. The final console keypress is exercised in a private test console.

{{fbdoc item="ex"}}
{{fbdoc item="filename" value="examples/manual/libraries/gsl1.bas"}}%%(freebasic)
'' examples/manual/libraries/gsl1.bas
''
'' Example extracted from the FreeBASIC Manual
'' from topic 'gsl, The GNU Scientific Library'
'' Purpose: Evaluate a power and a hypotenuse through GSL.
'' Responsibilities: Demonstrate the native double-valued math interface.
'' This example contains no allocated matrices or scientific solver state.
''
'' See Also: https://www.freebasic.net/wiki/wikka.php?wakka=ExtLibgsl
'' --------

'' Elementary math example
#include "gsl/gsl_math.bi"

'' Raise the value of 3.141 to the fourth power
' The D0 suffix supplies a Double literal without rounding through Single.
? "3.141 ^ 4 = "; gsl_pow_4(3.141D0)
?

'' Find the hypotenuse of a right triangle with sides 3 and 4
? "The hypotenuse of a right triangle with sides of length 3 and 4 is"; gsl_hypot(3, 4)
?

Sleep
'' end of gsl1.bas
%%

{{fbdoc item="filename" value="examples/manual/libraries/gsl2.bas"}}%%(freebasic)
'' examples/manual/libraries/gsl2.bas
''
'' Example extracted from the FreeBASIC Manual
'' from topic 'gsl, The GNU Scientific Library'
'' Purpose: Create, print and transpose a small GSL matrix.
'' Responsibilities: Check allocation and release the owned matrix.
'' This example contains no matrix views or shared backing storage.
''
'' See Also: https://www.freebasic.net/wiki/wikka.php?wakka=ExtLibgsl
'' --------

'' Matrix example
#include "gsl/gsl_matrix.bi"

'' gsl uses the c-style row major order, unlike VB or Fortran
? "A 3x3 matrix"
Dim As gsl_matrix Ptr m = gsl_matrix_alloc(3, 3)
If m = 0 Then
	Print "Could not allocate the GSL matrix"
	End 1
End If
For i As Integer = 0 To 2
	For j As Integer = 0 To 2
		gsl_matrix_set (m, i, j, 0.23D0 + 100*i + j)
	Next
Next

For i As Integer = 0 To 2
	For j As Integer = 0 To 2
		Print "m(";i;",";j;") = "; gsl_matrix_get (m, i, j)
	Next
Next
?

If gsl_matrix_transpose(m) <> GSL_SUCCESS Then
	gsl_matrix_free(m)
	Print "Could not transpose the GSL matrix"
	End 1
End If

? "And its transpose"
For i As Integer = 0 To 2
	For j As Integer = 0 To 2
		Print "m(";i;",";j;") = "; gsl_matrix_get (m, i, j)
	Next
Next

' The owning allocation is released before waiting for the final keypress.
gsl_matrix_free(m)
Sleep
'' end of gsl2.bas
%%

